.TH PORT_LOOKUP 2
.SH NAME
port_lookup, port_find \- find a named port (SYS_PORT_LOOKUP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long port_lookup(const char *name);
long port_find(const char *name);   /* LOOKUP_NOWAIT */
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B port_lookup
returns the number of the port called
.IR name .
If there is none yet, it waits until a server creates it, so programs
need not care in which order the servers start.
.B port_find
passes
.B LOOKUP_NOWAIT
and answers at once.
.PP
If the name is one the supervisor starts on demand (declared with
.BR sys_want (2)),
a waiting lookup also tells the supervisor, which starts the server; the
caller wakes when its port appears. If that server cannot start, the
supervisor fails the waiting lookups with its error.
.B port_find
never starts anything.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The port's number (> 0), or a negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B ENOENT
.RB ( port_find )
No port has this name.
.TP
.B EINVAL
.I name
is empty or longer than 15 characters.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I name
is not readable.
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal the process catches came while it waited.
.TP
.B EPIPE
The process is ending.
.PP
Any other error is the one the supervisor gave with
.BR sys_want (2)
for a server that cannot start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR port_create (2),
.BR ipc_call (2),
.BR sys_want (2),
.BR init (8)
